From: gstreamer-custom-build local patches
Subject: [PATCH] d3d11deinterlace: map the D3D11 memory around VideoProcessorBlt

d3d11deinterlace produces a picture that freezes on the very first frame.
VideoProcessorBlt() keeps returning S_OK for every buffer, the element keeps
pushing buffers at the right rate, and downstream keeps decoding them, but the
content never changes. It happens with every deinterlacing method (blend, bob,
adaptive and the default flagset), with any input, and is easy to reproduce
without a camera:

    gst-launch-1.0 videotestsrc pattern=ball ! video/x-raw,format=NV12 \
        ! interlace ! d3d11upload ! d3d11deinterlace ! d3d11download \
        ! videoconvert ! jpegenc ! multifilesink location=f_%03d.jpg

Every written JPEG is byte identical, while the same pipeline without
d3d11deinterlace produces the expected moving picture. Measured on an
RTX 3090 (NVIDIA 581.29) with GStreamer 1.28.5, but the cause is not
vendor specific.

gst_d3d11_deinterlace_transform() takes the ID3D11VideoProcessorInputView and
ID3D11VideoProcessorOutputView straight out of the GstD3D11Memory of the
input and output buffer and calls VideoProcessorBlt() on them, without ever
mapping either memory.

GstD3D11Memory keeps a staging texture next to the D3D11 texture and tracks
which of the two holds the up to date copy with the NEED_UPLOAD and
NEED_DOWNLOAD flags. Those flags are only maintained by gst_memory_map() and
gst_memory_unmap(): gst_d3d11_memory_map() with GST_MAP_D3D11 | GST_MAP_READ
performs the pending staging -> texture upload, and
gst_d3d11_memory_unmap_full() sets NEED_DOWNLOAD when the memory was mapped
with GST_MAP_D3D11 | GST_MAP_WRITE, so that the next CPU read copies the
texture back into the staging texture. Skipping the mapping therefore means:

  - a pending CPU write on the input is never uploaded, so the video processor
    reads a stale texture (this is what happens behind d3d11upload, which
    writes through the staging texture), and

  - the output is never flagged NEED_DOWNLOAD, so the next CPU read
    (d3d11download, gst_video_frame_copy(), the fallback path of another
    element, ...) keeps handing out the staging texture that was downloaded
    for the first frame. That is the frozen picture.

It also explains why the freeze is invisible to the element itself: the blit
really does run and really does write into the output texture. Only the
bookkeeping that tells the rest of the pipeline about it is missing. Setting
the destination rectangle to a quarter of the frame is reflected in the very
first output buffer and in no later one.

gst_d3d11_converter_do_processor_blt() in gst-libs/gst/d3d11/gstd3d11converter.cpp
already maps input and output around its VideoProcessorBlt() call, which is why
the video processor path of d3d11convert works. Do the same here.

The reference frames kept in the past/future queues are not mapped again: they
were mapped for reading while they were the current input buffer, so their
texture already holds the current copy.
diff --git a/_p2_/sys/d3d11/gstd3d11deinterlace.cpp b/_p2_/sys/d3d11/gstd3d11deinterlace.cpp
index 4d4c17a94e..db52a890f5 100644
--- a/_p2_/sys/d3d11/gstd3d11deinterlace.cpp
+++ b/_p2_/sys/d3d11/gstd3d11deinterlace.cpp
@@ -1669,6 +1669,8 @@ gst_d3d11_deinterlace_transform (GstBaseTransform * trans, GstBuffer * inbuf,
   ID3D11VideoProcessorInputView *past_surfaces[MAX_NUM_REFERENCES] = { NULL, };
   guint future_frames = 0;
   guint past_frames = 0;
+  GstMemory *in_mem, *out_mem;
+  GstMapInfo in_map, out_map;
   HRESULT hr;
   guint i;
 
@@ -1778,12 +1780,51 @@ gst_d3d11_deinterlace_transform (GstBaseTransform * trans, GstBuffer * inbuf,
     proc_stream.ppPastSurfaces = past_surfaces;
   }
 
-  GstD3D11DeviceLockGuard lk (self->device);
-  self->video_context->VideoProcessorSetStreamFrameFormat (self->video_proc, 0,
-      frame_foramt);
+  /* Map the D3D11 memory around VideoProcessorBlt(), the same way
+   * gst_d3d11_converter_do_processor_blt() does.
+   *
+   * GstD3D11Memory keeps a staging texture next to the D3D11 texture and uses
+   * the NEED_UPLOAD / NEED_DOWNLOAD flags to know which of the two holds the
+   * up to date copy. Those flags are only maintained by gst_memory_map() /
+   * gst_memory_unmap(). Without the mapping here:
+   *
+   *   - a pending CPU write on the input (d3d11upload writes through the
+   *     staging texture) is never uploaded, so the processor reads a stale
+   *     texture, and
+   *   - the output is never flagged NEED_DOWNLOAD, so the next CPU read
+   *     (d3d11download, gst_video_frame_copy(), ...) keeps returning the
+   *     staging texture from the previous download.
+   *
+   * The visible result is a picture that freezes on the first frame while
+   * VideoProcessorBlt() keeps returning S_OK, i.e. deinterlacing appears to
+   * do nothing at all. */
+  in_mem = gst_buffer_peek_memory (inbuf, 0);
+  out_mem = gst_buffer_peek_memory (outbuf, 0);
+
+  if (!gst_memory_map (in_mem, &in_map,
+          (GstMapFlags) (GST_MAP_READ | GST_MAP_D3D11))) {
+    GST_ERROR_OBJECT (self, "Couldn't map input buffer");
+    return GST_FLOW_ERROR;
+  }
+
+  if (!gst_memory_map (out_mem, &out_map,
+          (GstMapFlags) (GST_MAP_WRITE | GST_MAP_D3D11))) {
+    GST_ERROR_OBJECT (self, "Couldn't map output buffer");
+    gst_memory_unmap (in_mem, &in_map);
+    return GST_FLOW_ERROR;
+  }
+
+  {
+    GstD3D11DeviceLockGuard lk (self->device);
+    self->video_context->VideoProcessorSetStreamFrameFormat (self->video_proc,
+        0, frame_foramt);
+
+    hr = self->video_context->VideoProcessorBlt (self->video_proc, pov, 0,
+        1, &proc_stream);
+  }
 
-  hr = self->video_context->VideoProcessorBlt (self->video_proc, pov, 0,
-      1, &proc_stream);
+  gst_memory_unmap (out_mem, &out_map);
+  gst_memory_unmap (in_mem, &in_map);
 
   if (!gst_d3d11_result (hr, self->device)) {
     GST_ERROR_OBJECT (self, "Failed to perform deinterlacing");
